{
  "abstract": "Let $X$ be a projective hyper-Kähler fourfold of $K3^{[2]}$-type whose transcendental Hodge structure has endomorphism field $\\mathbb Q$, and let $\\Sigma\\subset X$ be a smooth K3 surface on which the symplectic form does not vanish. Restriction to $\\Sigma$ multiplies the Beauville–Bogomolov form on $T(X)$ by a positive integer $c$, the multiplier of $\\Sigma$. When $T(X)$ is the transcendental lattice of a K3 surface $S$, a non-square multiplier gives an algebraic Hodge similarity $T(S)_{\\mathbb Q}\\to T(\\Sigma)_{\\mathbb Q}$ that is not a scaled isometry, and the Hodge conjecture for $S\\times\\Sigma$. Generalized Nikulin surfaces give $c=2$ on families of dimension $12$. We show that this mechanism gives only squares at small Picard number. If $\\rho(X)=1$, then $X$ contains no smooth K3 surface. If $\\rho(X)=2$, then $c\\in\\{1,4,16\\}$. For Hilbert squares $S^{[2]}$ with $\\rho(S)=1$, $c=4$. The proofs combine the self-intersection formula $[\\Sigma]^2=\\int_\\Sigma c_2(X)-24$ with Fujiki's relations, an explicit description of $H^4(X,\\mathbb Z)$, a determinant identity for the restricted Néron–Severi form, and one positivity condition: the restricted form minus $c$ times the Beauville–Bogomolov form is non-negative on nef classes. The cohomological conditions alone leave $c=19$ on moduli spaces $M_S(2,h,28)$ over K3 surfaces of degree $114$. There we construct an integral Hodge class and a K3 surface of degree $6$ that satisfy all of them. The positivity condition, with the Bayer–Macrì description of nef cones, excludes this case. For immersed surfaces with double points the bound on $c$ disappears.",
